Z||000000||84se48||05-20-2001||03:07 PM||||Hi guys an gals, problem: 84se 2.5-- tried to tune up my car. New plugs,wires, cap,rotor,air filter,pcv and filter. Any way, after I did all this, I jumped the ALCU. The VECI says"8 btdc in drive" Ok, timing was 14 #1, about 17 #4 . I turned dis. Got 8 #1,12#4, I moved it a little more and I got 8-10-#1,10-12#4. When do you think she will blow? I don't seem to be able to get it any closer than is. Do I have a problem? HELP!!!||||reg||9
Z||000001||Taijiguy||05-20-2001||04:18 PM||||Am I reading your post right? You're trying to get some timing reference from #4? I've never heard of using anything but #1 for timing purposes. My suggestion is to trash the light. You have no way of knowing if the outer part of the balancer has moved or not. I've always just advanced the timing until the rpm <I>just</I> starts to drop off, then back up until it smooths out, and then back to where it starts to drop off again. Test drive and make sure no pinging under acceleration. If there is, back off a touch and test drive it again. That's the best way I've ever found to time ANY car... ||||reg||1
Z||000002||btoth||05-20-2001||04:26 PM||||The service manuals say to take the avg. of the #1 and #4 (for the V6 anyway).<P>I always just go by #1... the computer should straighten out the timing itself if it's close enough.||||reg||1
Z||000003||84se48||05-20-2001||05:11 PM||||Trash the light? True, I've always used # 1 to set timing on any other engine but the Haynes manual and the sticker on the deck lid say to do it this way.Seems to run good but has a little ping to it,was worse were it was. I,ll try using #1 only. thanks||||reg||5
